<!-- cell: title-page id="694987f8-7e3e-49e4-8a4b-67f0df95aebd" title="Dry Fork" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="7163483c-e656-47f5-80fc-cdda3bb0ab50" -->

The creek at Dry Fork stopped running on the ninth of July, and by the twelfth Hattie Crane had lost four steers to the heat.

She rode up the valley at dawn with her son Eli and found the reason: a wall of rock and timber across the creek bed, two miles above her land, and behind it a long brown lake glittering on Silas Boone's side of the fence. Two of Boone's riders sat their horses beside it with rifles across their knees.

"He's done it to the Mendozas too," said Eli. "And old Pryor. He wants the whole valley, Ma. He'll dry us out and buy us cheap."

"I know what he wants."

That night Eli cleaned his father's rifle at the kitchen table. Hattie watched him for a while, then took it from him and put it back over the door.

"Your father died on the end of one of those," she said. "I'm not burying you on the end of another."

Instead she took down the tin box from the top shelf, where Ben had kept the deed and the survey he had paid for in '81, when the territory was still arguing about where anything began. Then she wrote out a copy in her neatest hand, and in the morning she sent Eli south to Tucson with it, to the territorial marshal.

"Ride hard," she told him. "And don't stop for anybody wearing Boone's brand."

Two days later she rode alone to the Boone ranch.

Silas Boone received her on his porch, a broad, pleasant man who smiled with everything except his eyes. "Mrs. Crane. Sorry to hear about your stock. My offer for your land still stands."

She unfolded the survey on his porch rail and held it flat against the wind. "The upper bend of the creek, where your dam sits, is on my land. Ben paid a surveyor two hundred dollars to say so. You built your wall on Crane property, Mr. Boone, and you've been stealing my water with it."

Boone stopped smiling. "Paper burns, Mrs. Crane."

"It does," said Hattie. "That's why there's a copy in Tucson with the territorial marshal, and my boy is bringing him here."

For a long moment the only sound was the wind in the cottonwoods. Then Boone turned to his foreman. "Open the dam."

The creek came down the valley that evening, brown and fast and loud, past the Mendozas and old Pryor and into the dry bed below the Crane house. Hattie stood in it up to her ankles and let it run over her boots.

Eli came home a week later with the marshal and a sunburn, and a story he would tell for the rest of his life.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="db8ce017-be90-4db5-a905-7c9dc1622b44" title="Hattie Crane" words="0-24, 126-143, 418-432" -->

A widowed rancher who faces down a cattle baron with a survey instead of a rifle.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="7c7c7848-d27e-4203-bb5e-ac7ab377ea50" title="Silas Boone" words="250-267, 339-345, 381-389" -->

The cattle baron who dams the creek to dry out his neighbours and buy their land cheap.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="efff94b3-6dc1-4e55-a303-93269269539e" title="Dry Fork" words="25-69, 368-380, 390-417" -->

The valley in the Arizona Territory whose creek runs past Boone's land, the Mendozas, old Pryor and the Crane house.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ffe33164-f941-449a-9dc0-243d195f24b6" title="Greed" words="84-109, 276-282, 322-338" -->

Boone's hunger for the whole valley, pursued by stealing its water.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b4bebf3c-a3a1-4b9a-b7ac-553656a52d42" title="Courage" words="240-249, 283-297, 346-367" -->

Hattie riding alone to Boone's ranch and calling his bluff.

<!-- cell: plot id="f5f2a0c0-fba5-4197-9231-f6b9bac777d9" title="The dam" words="0-24, 25-69, 165-200, 240-249, 283-338, 339-345, 346-367, 381-389, 390-417" -->

Boone dams the creek and Hattie's cattle die; she finds her late husband's survey proving the dam is on her land, confronts Boone with it and with a copy held by the marshal, and he opens the dam.

<!-- cell: plot id="7890b4f1-9608-4901-a882-53b18c06a1a8" title="Eli and the rifle" words="115-143, 144-164, 201-226, 227-239, 433-456" -->

Hattie's son wants to fight with his father's rifle; she takes it from him and sends him to Tucson with a copy of the survey instead, and he comes home with the marshal.
